\title{Special sets}
\date{2026-02-18T12:39:02Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{There are various sets that are so fundamental and frequently useful that we give them special names.}

\ul{
  \li{#{\varnothing}, the empty set}
  \li{#{\N = \{0, 1, 2, \dots\}}, the natural numbers}
  \li{#{\Z = \{\dots, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, \dots\}}, the integers}
  \li{#{\Z^+ = \{1, 2, \dots\}}, the positive integers}
  \li{#{\Q = \{ p/q \mid p \in \Z, q \in \Z, q \neq 0 \}}, the rational numbers
   \p{Actually, the definition of the rational numbers is a bit more subtle than this; the above definition does not take into account the fact that, say, #{1/2 = 2/4}.  We will see how to deal with this when we learn about equivalence relations in a couple weeks.}
  }
  \li{\R, the real numbers}
  \li{\C, the complex numbers}
}
\p{We will not use \C at all in this class, and we will only mention \R occasionally.}
